File Index

/client.ts
Purpose: SEAMLESS SIGN-IN: tryEnrollDeviceSilently() binds this browser with no prompt when a hub session already exists (never throws — it runs inside the access probe, where an exception would abort the whole check and leave the page stuck, the failure that once surfaced as a misleading "server unreachable"), and authSignInUrl() builds the hub login URL with a ?returnTo back here for when there is no session yet. Between them the user pastes nothing and manages only the auth.* login. Thin fetch wrappers for mud-builder-server (capabilities, areas incl. baseHash/conditional save, reload, world incl. Phase 12b limitPressure, skills/groups incl. Phase 12 baseHash conditional overlay saves, Phase 9 auth key management, Phase 10 import preview/commit + audit tail, Phase 11 presence/presenceBeat + ExternalRef/PresenceEntry types, Phase 12 areaMap/worldMap + map response types now carrying exit locks + room warps, Phase 13 spawn(file) → SimulateResetsResult imported straight from @shatteredarchive/merc-area, not duplicated locally, Phase 14a codegenSpells/saveCodegenSpells (baseHash-conditional) + codegenPatch via a requestText helper because the patch route answers text/plain and the JSON-only request<T> would silently mangle it, Phase 15 rebuildStatus()/triggerRebuild() + RebuildStatus/RebuildStatusResponse types mirroring the server's rebuild-store.ts, Capabilities.rebuildEnabled, Phase 14c stateRefresh()/stateLive() + LiveStateResponse wrapping merc-area's LiveSnapshot, Phase G rolesMe()/rolesList()/setRole() + RolesMe/RoleGrant/ServiceTier types mirroring the server's role-store.ts ladder, snippets()/saveSnippets() + Snippet/SnippetKind types — the latter two are whole-collection (GET/PUT), not per-item, matching the server's actual shape) — attaches a Bearer credential on every request; errors throw ApiError with status, 401s hint at the Access tab. 2026-07-30 (device-bound credentials): the localStorage 'mb-token' is GONE. Two tiers now — tier 1 is device credentials via @shatteredarchive/sdk-client (non-extractable keypair in IndexedDB, short-lived per-service tokens held in memory), tier 2 is a manually pasted master/API/account key kept in MEMORY ONLY (break-glass: first-run bootstrap, CI, auth-server unreachable — so losing it on reload is intended, NOT a bug to fix by re-persisting). authToken() prefers the device token and swallows NeedsEnrollmentError to '' so a dead enrolment becomes an ordinary 401 the UI already renders, not an unhandled rejection in every caller. configureDeviceCredentials(authPublicUrl) is fed from /api/capabilities because the bundle ships no VITE_ build args, and it FAILS SOFT rather than throwing, reporting a REASON via deviceUnavailableReason() so the UI can say what to do instead of silently omitting the panel. Two guarded cases: a non-secure context (WebCrypto is secure-context-only, judged by scheme+HOSTNAME and never by the resolved IP — so http://localhost is trusted but http://any.hostname is not, even pointing at 127.0.0.1) and absent/blocked IndexedDB. NEITHER is the normal path here: every service is reached by hostname through the nginx router over https, dev included (tls-dev.conf + an mkcert cert covering *.shatteredarchive.dev), so device credentials work in dev exactly as in prod — the guards are for off-path access. Missing IndexedDB disables the feature deliberately, since a key that cannot persist would re-prompt enrolment every reload. Simulacrum plan (2026-08-16): the `ServiceTier` type + `SERVICE_TIERS` literal array here are an un-imported copy of services-server's auth-tiers.ts ladder (this client has no direct dependency on that package) — both gained the new `builder` tier between `manager` and `trusted`; keep this array in sync by hand if the source ladder changes again. Same day: `RolesMe` gained `accountId` and (hours later, superseding it as the grant form's primary field) `username` — see features/roles/.annotated for the live bugs these close. `setRole()` changed shape the same day: `(username, tier)` posting to `/api/roles`, not `(accountId, tier, username)` posting to `/api/roles/:accountId` — the server resolves the username to a real accountId itself now (roles.ts).
